WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.496
Pattern 3: background jobs and queues.

2
00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:16.792
Some work takes longer than anyone should hold an HTTP request open: summarizing a long document set, running an agent that makes dozens of tool calls, generating a report with several model passes and checks.

3
00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:19.333
For these, split the request from the work.

4
00:00:19.613 --> 00:00:32.093
The endpoint validates the request, writes a job record, puts a message on a queue, and returns immediately with a job id (HTTP 202 Accepted is the conventional status).

5
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:39.477
A separate worker process takes jobs off the queue, calls the model, stores the result, and updates the job’s status.

6
00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:47.331
The client polls a status endpoint, subscribes to a stream of progress events, or gets a notification when the job finishes.

7
00:00:47.611 --> 00:00:49.125
This buys you three things.

8
00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:55.208
Durations are no longer bounded by your web host’s request limit (lesson 3 has the numbers).

9
00:00:55.488 --> 00:01:05.600
Failures become recoverable: a worker that crashes leaves the job in the queue for another worker, and a job that failed can be retried later without the user resubmitting.

10
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:14.499
And load becomes smooth: a spike of requests becomes a longer queue rather than a wall of concurrent model calls that trips your rate limits.

11
00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:18.725
It also brings a new obligation: the same job can run twice.

12
00:01:19.005 --> 00:01:25.981
Queues typically deliver at least once, and a worker can crash after the model answered but before it recorded success.

13
00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:28.501
Calling the model twice only costs money.

14
00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:32.707
Sending the customer two emails, or creating two tickets, is a bug.

15
00:01:32.987 --> 00:01:46.043
Make every side effect at the end of a job idempotent, with an idempotency key derived from the job id, the same pattern Stripe’s API uses for payments and the one you used for write tools in Studio 1.

16
00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:57.587
And because the worker runs unattended, the checks you’d do by eye in a chat UI (schema validation, evidence checks, a judge pass) must run in code before any side effect.
